llmlang:
	lockfile.py:
		LockEntry (stores, per source-handled entry, its file, text hash, and code hash):
			- stores, per source-handled entry, its file, text hash, and code hash
			- may also store a last-change disposition, bound to the entry's spec hash (its own text combined with every currently applicable policy's text) and code hash it was recorded against

		TestTrace (stores, per linked test comment, the explanation text and backing code hash):
			- stores, per linked test comment, the explanation text, relative path, and backing code hash

		Finding data (stores one check/coverage failure as structured data, not just display text):
			- category: text ("CODE_DIVERGED"): a fixed, stable identifier for the kind of failure, decoupled from the human-readable message's exact wording
			- message: text ("the code changed with no matching llmlang update - investigate before assuming it was intentional"): the full human-readable explanation, identical to what was printed before this existed
			- file: text ("legacy_app/notes.py") or nothing: the file the failure is about, when known
			- line: number (12) or nothing: the line within that file, when known

		LOCKFILE_SCHEMA_VERSION (the lockfile's own schema version, bumped whenever its shape changes):
			- the lockfile's own schema version, bumped whenever its shape changes

		RULESET_VERSION (the ruleset version every entry in a lockfile is built under together):
			- the ruleset version every entry in a lockfile is built under together

	parser.py:
		parse (parses a tab-indented llmlang file into a tree of folders, files, classes, and entries):
			- accepts a file path
			- parses a tab-indented llmlang file into a tree of folders, files, classes, named entries, and data entries
			- a source-handled entry header must include a parenthesized summary after the identifier and before the colon
			- a data entry header must include the data marker after the identifier and before the parenthesized summary
			- a nested regular bullet belongs to the same nearest entry, file or class bullet group, or policy item as its parent
			- a test bullet can live under a named entry, file, or class node
			- a header's real name is always its first word; any words after a folder, file, or class name up to the colon are a freetext hint, discarded once the name is extracted
			- a bare `@entry-point` or `@entry-point(label)` line immediately before a named entry header marks that entry as a flow-generation starting point, rejecting it outright on any other kind of header
			- a `→ call CanonicalName` or `→ call operation via ExternalService` bullet, and an optional following `← return ...` bullet, are valid anywhere a regular bullet is valid, including nested under a decision, case, or repeat bullet
			- records the source line number of each folder, file, class, named entry, and data entry header, and of each test bullet
			- returns the parsed tree

	extractor.py:
		TestComment (stores a linked llm-test comment and the backing code it anchors):
			- stores a linked llm-test comment's canonical node, explanation text, relative path, source line, and backing code
			- exposes a code hash derived from the backing code

		extract_by_handle (extracts the code implementing an entry by locating its comment handle and taking everything from the handle's own comment line up to the next handle):
			- accepts a file path and canonical entry name
			- extracts the code implementing an entry by locating its comment handle and taking everything from the handle's own comment line up to the next handle
			- including the comment line itself means the entry's summary comment is part of the resulting hash, so it can't drift from the llmlang summary undetected
			- returns the extracted code and the line number of its handle comment, or nothing if the handle isn't found

		handle_line_numbers (finds the source line of every comment handle in a file, for coverage checking):
			- accepts a file path
			- finds the source line of every comment handle in a file, for coverage checking
			- returns the set of line numbers

		test_comment_roots (chooses where llm-test comments are scanned):
			- accepts an llmlang file path
			- returns the llmlang file directory and the repository tests directory when a repository root is found

		test_comment_base (chooses the stable base path for test trace paths):
			- accepts an llmlang file path
			- returns the repository root when one is found, otherwise the llmlang file directory

		test_comments_by_node (collects llm-test comments by canonical node name):
			- accepts one or more root paths
			- scans source-like text files under the root paths for llm-test handles
			- records each llm-test comment's canonical node, exact explanation text, relative path, source line, and backing test code
			- returns a mapping of canonical node name to linked test comment records

	flow.py:
		generate_flow (composes a .llmflow view by walking every @entry-point entry and inlining each → call target's own bullets, recursively, expanding each referenced entry only the first time it's reached in a given trace):
			- accepts the list of @entry-point entries and every named entry's own summary and bullets
			- composes a .llmflow view by walking every @entry-point entry and inlining each → call target's own bullets, recursively
			- expands a referenced entry's own bullets only the first time it's reached in a given entry point's trace; a repeat reference (including a cycle) renders as a bare call line with nothing nested under it
			- resolves a → call target to a known llmlang entry by exact canonical name or unique suffix match
			- treats a → call target containing " via " as an external call with nothing to resolve or expand
			- renders a plain "returns ..." bullet with a ← marker instead of -, the same as an explicit ← return line
			- renders a called entry's own trailing "returns ..."/← return bullet at the calling → call line's own depth, not nested inside the called entry's body, since it marks control coming back out to the caller's level
			- reports each non-external → call target that doesn't resolve to exactly one entry as an error naming the entry it came from, rather than raising or losing any other output
			- returns the composed text for every entry point, and the list of any call-target errors found

		generate_flow_for_tree (builds the entry-point list and entry content from a parsed llmlang tree, then composes the flow view):
			- accepts a parsed llmlang tree
			- builds the entry-point list and entry content from a parsed llmlang tree, then composes the flow view using generate_flow
			- excludes ~ test bullets from every entry's content; a flow view is about execution, not test coverage
			- returns the same (text, errors) shape as generate_flow

	lockfile_builder.py:
		build (builds a lockfile by using Parser to read llmlang, Extractor to locate each entry's code, and Lockfile to record a text and code hash per entry):
			- accepts an llmlang file path and a lockfile output path
			- builds a lockfile by using Parser to read llmlang, Extractor to locate each source-handled entry's code, and Lockfile to record a text and code hash per entry
			- writes test trace records with the llm-test explanation text, relative path, and backing code hash
			- generates and overwrites the sibling .llmflow file from the current @entry-point entries and bullets
			- checks whether any test bullet lacks a matching llm-test comment or any llm-test comment lacks a matching test bullet:
				- if yes, refuses to write anything
			- checks whether any → call target in an @entry-point entry's reachable bullets is unresolved or ambiguous:
				- if yes, refuses to write anything
			- has no notion of dispositions or a change manifest at all; never reads or writes anything but the lockfile and the generated flow file
			- reproduces the same hashes when run again against an unchanged codebase
			- returns the built lock data

		finalize (guards an incremental rebuild against silently skipping a flagged entry, reading and updating a change manifest bound to the exact spec and code hashes it describes):
			- accepts an llmlang file path, a lockfile output path, and a change manifest file path
			- uses Checker to find every named entry currently flagged against the existing lockfile, and refuses to write anything unless the change manifest covers every one of them with a non-empty disposition
			- checks whether any → call target in an @entry-point entry's reachable bullets is unresolved or ambiguous, same as build:
				- if yes, refuses to write anything
			- generates and overwrites the sibling .llmflow file from the current @entry-point entries and bullets, same as build; a flow-only edit needs no disposition of its own, since it can only exist as part of an entry's own bullets, already gated by that entry's own SPEC_DIVERGED
			- accepts a fresh disposition in the manifest as a plain string, and stamps it with the entry's current spec hash and code hash
			- a spec hash combines the entry's own text with every policy currently covering it, so a disposition goes stale whether the entry's own text changed or an applicable policy did
			- for a manifest entry that's already stamped, keeps it only if both hashes still match the entry's current state; otherwise drops it as stale, along with any entry whose key no longer exists
			- builds every hash and disposition fresh from the current llmlang text, current code, current policies, and current manifest only; never carries anything forward from the lockfile's own prior content
			- writes both the new lockfile and the updated (stamped and pruned) manifest file, but never alters a disposition's own text
			- returns the built lock data

	coverage_checker.py:
		check_coverage (checks every module- and class-level Python function against comment handles and inline llm-exempt markers, reporting each uncovered one as UNMAPPED_CODE):
			- accepts an llmlang file path
			- checks every module- and class-level Python function against comment handles and inline llm-exempt markers, reporting each uncovered one as UNMAPPED_CODE
			- only checks files with a Python extension; files in other languages are silently skipped, not flagged
			- a function is covered if a comment handle sits on the line directly above it, or above its first decorator
			- a function nested inside another function is never checked on its own; it has no stable qualified name to report or exempt, and its enclosing function's own handle (if any) already sweeps its code into that handle's region
			- a function is exempt if a bare "[llm-exempt]" comment sits on the line directly above it, or above its first decorator - a reason may be written before the tag on the same line but isn't required, and there is no built-in exemption for dunder methods, private helpers, or anything else
			- returns the list of findings, each a Finding record

	lockfile_checker.py:
		check (checks a lockfile by using Parser and Extractor to compare current text and code hashes against Lockfile, reporting each entry as SPEC_DIVERGED, CODE_DIVERGED, or unchanged, alongside its file and line):
			- accepts an llmlang file path and a lockfile path
			- checks a lockfile by using Parser and Extractor to compare current text and code hashes against Lockfile, reporting each entry as SPEC_DIVERGED, CODE_DIVERGED, or unchanged, alongside its file and line
			- checks whether any test bullet lacks a matching llm-test comment with the same text and canonical node name:
				- if yes, reports TEST_LINK_MISSING
			- checks whether any llm-test comment lacks a matching test bullet with the same text and canonical node name:
				- if yes, reports TEST_TRACE_STALE
			- checks whether a linked test's backing code hash differs from the lockfile:
				- if yes, reports TEST_CODE_DIVERGED
			- checks whether a → call target reachable from an @entry-point entry is unresolved or ambiguous:
				- if yes, reports FLOW_ERROR, naming the entry it came from, without losing any other finding for the same file
			- checks whether the checked-in .llmflow matches what generating one fresh from the current llmlang and code would produce:
				- if no, reports FLOW_FILE_STALE
			- checks whether an entry's own llmlang text changed, it's brand new, or an upstream policy covering it changed:
				- if yes, reports it as SPEC_DIVERGED
			- checks whether an entry's handle is missing from the code or its code changed unexpectedly:
				- if yes, reports it as CODE_DIVERGED
			- reports a codebase with none of these changes as fully clean
			- never prints anything itself; every finding is returned as a Finding record in a list, so a caller can present every failure from a run together instead of relying on interleaved prints, or render them as structured data instead of text
			- each finding names a file and line where known: entry-level findings point at the entry's header line in the llmlang file, code-level findings point at the handle's own comment line in the source file
			- returns whether everything checked out clean, the set of named-entry tracking keys currently flagged for any reason, and the list of findings

	cli.py:
		find_pyproject (finds the nearest pyproject.toml by walking up from a starting directory):
			- accepts a starting directory
			- finds the nearest pyproject.toml by walking up from a starting directory, all the way to the filesystem root
			- returns the first pyproject.toml found, or nothing

		load_config (loads the [tool.llmlang] table from pyproject.toml, degrading gracefully when it can't be read):
			- accepts a starting directory and an optional explicit config path override
			- loads the [tool.llmlang] table from pyproject.toml, degrading gracefully when it can't be read
			- checks whether an override path was given:
				- if yes, uses it directly
				- if no, finds the nearest pyproject.toml from the starting directory
			- checks whether the override path doesn't exist, or this Python has no tomllib (needs 3.11+):
				- if yes, warns to stderr and returns an empty config, rather than crashing
			- checks whether no pyproject.toml was found at all:
				- if yes, falls back to an empty config, with no warning
			- returns the [tool.llmlang] table

		resolve_coverage (decides whether --coverage is active, letting a CLI flag override the config either way):
			- accepts the parsed flags and the loaded config
			- decides whether --coverage is active, letting a CLI flag override the config either way
			- --no-coverage always forces it off, then --coverage always forces it on, then the config's own coverage value is the default
			- returns whether coverage checking is active

		discover_llm_files (finds every *.llm file under a directory, honoring the built-in and config-configured exclusions):
			- accepts a root directory and an extra set of directory names to exclude
			- finds every *.llm file under a directory, honoring the built-in and config-configured exclusions
			- excludes .git, __pycache__, node_modules, venv, and .venv by default, plus anything the config's exclude list adds
			- returns the sorted list of discovered files

		check_one (checks a single llmlang file, adding coverage findings when coverage is active):
			- accepts an llmlang file path and whether coverage checking is active
			→ call llmlang.lockfile_checker.check
			- if checking raises an error, reports it as a single ERROR finding instead of letting the exception propagate
			- checks whether coverage checking is active:
				- if yes:
					→ call llmlang.coverage_checker.check_coverage
				- if no, skips coverage checking
			- returns whether it checked out clean and the combined list of findings

		print_report_text (prints every finding across every checked file in one delineated FAILURES section, or a bare OK line when nothing was flagged):
			- accepts every checked file's findings, keyed by file
			- prints every finding across every checked file in one delineated FAILURES section, or a bare OK line when nothing was flagged
			- reports the total failure and file count at the end, correctly pluralized

		print_report_json (prints every finding as JSON, structured identically to what the text report renders from):
			- accepts every checked file's findings, keyed by file
			- prints every finding as JSON, structured identically to what the text report renders from
			- the JSON shape is {"ok": bool, "results": {file: [{category, message, file, line}, ...]}}

		run_check (checks every path given, applying config-resolved coverage and exclusions, and reports every failure together):
			- accepts a list of file or directory paths, the parsed flags, an optional config path override, and the output format
			- checks every path given, applying config-resolved coverage and exclusions, and reports every failure together
			→ call llmlang.cli.load_config
			→ call llmlang.cli.resolve_coverage
			- accepts any number of file or directory paths, auto-discovering every *.llm file under a directory and checking a .llm file directly
			→ call llmlang.cli.discover_llm_files
			- warns to stderr and skips a path that doesn't exist, rather than failing the whole run
			- exits with an error if no .llm files are found under any given path
			- repeats checking for each discovered or directly given llmlang file:
				→ call llmlang.cli.check_one
			- prints "checking X" progress and any warnings to stderr, so stdout stays clean for the report in either format
			- for json case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.print_report_json
			- for text case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.print_report_text
			- exits 0 if every checked file was clean, 1 otherwise

		run_build (builds a fresh lockfile for exactly one llmlang file, unguarded):
			- accepts a single llmlang file path
			- builds a fresh lockfile for exactly one llmlang file, unguarded
			- refuses more than one file argument, since build is always scoped to one project
			→ call llmlang.lockfile_builder.build
			- exits 1 and prints the error on failure

		run_finalize (finalizes exactly one llmlang file's lockfile and change manifest, guarded by Checker):
			- accepts a single llmlang file path
			- finalizes exactly one llmlang file's lockfile and change manifest, guarded by Checker
			- refuses more than one file argument, since finalize is always scoped to one project
			→ call llmlang.lockfile_builder.finalize
			- exits 1 and prints the error on failure

		@entry-point(llmlang <command>: the compiler's own CLI entry point)
		main (parses argv into a command and validated flags, then dispatches to check, build, or finalize):
			- accepts no arguments; reads argv directly
			- parses argv into a command and validated flags, then dispatches to check, build, or finalize
			- --help or -h anywhere in argv, or no arguments at all, prints usage
			- --version prints the installed package version, falling back to a bundled version string when llmlang isn't actually installed
			- rejects a command outside {check, build, finalize}, and rejects any flag that isn't valid for the given command (--coverage on build, say, or --config on finalize) - nothing is silently ignored
			- --config and --output-format each consume the next argument as their value
			- for check case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.run_check
			- for build case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.run_build
			- for finalize case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.run_finalize
